# LinkBench (Facebook) -- the 2-table social-graph schema, authored in the shared DSL. One wide Node
# record (a data blob + metadata) and a directed link carrying its own data. The real LinkBench schema
# (github.com/facebookarchive/linkbench): nodes id,type,version,time,data; links id1,link_type,id2,
# visibility,version,time,data, keyed by (id1, link_type, id2). `id` is the vid and `type` is renamed
# `ntype` (type is a DSL keyword).
#
# ONE EDGE TYPE PER LINKBENCH LINK TYPE: a link's identity includes its link_type, and
# LinkBench's default config generates 2 types over the same pairs (2,416,553 of 2,832,817 pairs at 1M carry
# both). Here an edge is identified by (src, etype, dst), so ONE `link` edge type with link_type as a property
# collapsed those pairs and dropped 46% of the links. Mapping: link1 = link_type 123456789, link2 = 123456790
# (loader.LINK_TYPES). Every LinkBench read names its link type (getLinkList(id1, link_type)), so it reads one
# type's list.
#
# Links are stored OUT only (keyed by id1), as in LinkBench's link table: every LinkBench read walks id1's
# out-links, so a reverse list would be write cost nothing reads.
# No property index: getNode is a point read, getLinkList an edge-range read. See experiments/linkbench/README.md.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- vertices ----
vertex Node prefix=node
    ntype    int
    version  int
    time     int
    data     bytes

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- edges ----
edge link1
    Node->Node
    visibility int
    version    int
    time       int
    data       bytes

edge link2
    Node->Node
    visibility int
    version    int
    time       int
    data       bytes
